{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat AGENTS.md && cat reviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-analysis.md'","aggregated_output":"# Working in this repository\n\nAxylo is a browser charting library. The product is specified in `docs/02-product-spec.md`; the build plan is `docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md`. Read the sections your task names before writing code.\n\n## Rules for every change\n\n- **Contracts are fixed.** `packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts` and `packages/axylo/src/public.ts` are the interfaces between modules. Do not edit them. If your work needs a change there, stop that part, finish what you can, and describe the exact change you need in your report.\n- **Stay in your files.** Each task lists the directories it owns. Do not create, edit or delete anything else, including configuration and other modules. Several agents work in this checkout at the same time.\n- **No runtime dependencies.** Use the platform (`Intl`, typed arrays). Dev dependencies already installed: TypeScript, Vitest, fast-check, esbuild, Playwright (system Chrome), axe-core. The network is not available; do not try to install anything.\n- **No DOM at import time.** A module that needs the DOM takes it as a parameter or touches it inside a function. Pure stages (data, scales, formatting, layout, scene) must run in Node.\n- **Hot paths use typed arrays.** No object per data point, no array of arrays per data point, no copying of large columns that the contracts say are borrowed.\n- **Never guess silently.** Anything that cannot be drawn as given is reported through the `DiagnosticSink` with the code from spec section 3.5.\n- **Tests come from the spec.** Expected values are derived from the specification and the fixture data, never by running the implementation and pasting its output. Use fast-check property tests for invariants (\"for any data …\"). A test you cannot make pass is reported as failing; do not weaken it or change its expected value.\n- **TypeScript strict, no `any`** in exported signatures. `__DEV__` is a build-time boolean (true in tests).\n- **Comments say why**, not what. Names are plain words.\n- **Do not commit.** The coordinator commits.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\nnpm run typecheck      # tsc over the library, its tests, and the API examples in docs/api\nnpm test               # unit and property tests (Node)\nnpm run build          # packages/axylo/dist\nnpm run test:browser   # browser tests against the built bundle, in system Chrome\n```\n\n## Your report\n\nEnd with a short report: what you built (files), what you verified and how (paste the last lines of the test run), what is not done or not verified, any contract change you need, and any place where the spec was ambiguous and what you assumed. Keep \"ran and saw\" apart from \"expect\".\n<!-- Analyst: Codex, requested gpt-6.2-astra xhigh; observed gpt-6.2-astra, effort xhigh. Read-only analysis of the DX12 batch at release candidate 1e93128 (records in rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/, not in git). Saved by the coordinator. -->\n\n**Accept the batch as the DX12 release record for `1e93128`.** Both models passed 3/3 on each of four tasks, exceeding the owner’s 2/3 rule in every cell. I reconstructed the expected tables from the raw fixtures and matched all 96 saved Chrome tables, including the missing days. I found no materially wrong chart on either graded input. [DX12](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:234) · [Release rule](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/README.md:16)\n\n### What earned the passes\n\nTimes are original runner wall-clock seconds, rounded, in attempt order.\n\n| Task | What all six answers did | Codex | Claude |\n|---|---|---:|---:|\n| Daily visits | Explicitly declared the UTC midnights as `type: 'date'`. Axylo sorted the 27 observations and broke the stroke on March 6, 12 and 26. `codex-daily-visits-2` showed lowercase “visits”, which the case-insensitive header check reasonably accepts. [Answer](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-daily-visits-2-UbIDoe/answer/chart.js:7) · [Grade](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-daily-visits-2-UbIDoe/grade.json:33) | 48, 51, 58 | 78, 74, 70 |\n| Regional sales | Sorted a copy of the shuffled quarters and labelled three grouped series. Codex sorted the fixed-width quarter strings lexically. Claude parsed year and quarter. All declared `parse: 'number'`, although Axylo already converts these all-decimal-string columns. Removing `parse` in Claude’s control produced three AX104 hints. [Codex answer](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-regional-sales-1-DNPmkQ/answer/chart.js:4) · [Claude control](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-regional-sales-1-bUf5s8/transcript.jsonl:113) | 65, 63, 60 | 76, 83, 75 |\n| Signups | Read the actual key `signpus` and supplied the reader label “Signups”. The date-only strings work by default. All three Claude runs also tried `signups` as a negative control and got AX101. On the alternate input, Axylo showed the April 9 gap and disclosed one omission both visibly and in the accessibility tree. [Control](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-signups-1-xUIhVW/transcript.jsonl:93) · [Grade](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-signups-2-kmTdYO/grade.json:48) | 46, 68, 47 | 56, 53, 61 |\n| Daily revenue | Explicitly chose calendar dates and converted five decimal strings alongside two numbers. Axylo supplied the date order and May 4 gap. This trap required agent action: Claude’s raw-data control still reported five AX106 omissions with `parse: 'number'`. [Control](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-daily-revenue-1-Rt4jil/transcript.jsonl:83) · [Expected readings](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/fixtures/daily-revenue.expected.json:10) | 65, 75, 122 | 115, 121, 81 |\n\n**Revenue is where a careful reviewer should qualify the pass.** In a separate read-only probe, all three Codex converters silently turned `\"0x10\"` into 16. All three Claude converters accepted `\".5\"` despite describing their regex as Axylo’s grammar. Claude revenue 2 and 3 also rejected the valid `\"+2\"`. Both graded fixtures use ordinary decimals. A revenue alternate containing these cases would let **table rows** catch wrong values and **reader note** catch silent conversions or spurious omissions. [Codex conversion](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-daily-revenue-2-HBZvJU/answer/chart.js:7) · [Claude conversion](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-daily-revenue-2-jtKCOV/answer/chart.js:3) · [Axylo’s grammar](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/values.ts:6)\n\n### What the agents read\n\nBoth families used **Getting started**, **Your data**, **Line** or **Bar**, **Options**, and the Node `validate()` guidance. Getting started explicitly teaches all five traps. Codex searched broadly across the index, conformance, stability, diagnostic pages and published examples, then reconstructed the options in its Node checks. `codex-signups-2` inspected the package manifest and both production and development bundles. `codex-daily-revenue-3` inspected the manifest and production bundle after Node rejected a permission flag. Claude read the package manifest in all 12 attempts, sometimes the README and `dist/index.d.ts` or `public.d.ts`, and often validated the same exported options function used by `render`. [Getting started](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/apps/docs/pages/getting-started.md:41) · [Codex bundle checks](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-signups-2-ORZvKV/transcript.jsonl:28) · [Claude declaration lookup](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-daily-revenue-1-Rt4jil/transcript.jsonl:125)\n\nThree documentation changes follow directly from the transcripts.\n\n- **Your data / Options:** Give the exact decimal grammar and a mixed-field normalization example that preserves invalid values for diagnostics. The existing example covers all-string data. `claude-daily-revenue-2` repeatedly searched `parse`, then investigated whether “row getter” meant a field accessor. [Data guide](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/apps/docs/pages/data.md:118) · [Options entry](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/apps/docs/pages/options.md:136) · [Transcript](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-daily-revenue-2-jtKCOV/transcript.jsonl:107)\n- **Published HTML, especially Options:** Make individual reference rows searchable as text. Long matching lines were omitted, and all 12 Claude attempts encountered a denied HTML-stripping command before continuing with file tools. Codex sales 2 and 3 searched literal closing tags to find the shared-axis `value` message. [Claude search result](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-daily-revenue-2-jtKCOV/transcript.jsonl:103) · [Codex lookup](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-regional-sales-3-JikJY0/transcript.jsonl:30)\n- **Diagnostics / Node validation:** Put a runnable plain-JavaScript check beside the TypeScript examples and say explicitly that `validate()` returns hints in production too. Codex searched for the invocation. Claude sales 2 ran both builds and switched to a standalone `check.mjs` after its shell loop was denied. [Diagnostics](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/apps/docs/pages/diagnostics.md:59) · [Codex search](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-daily-visits-1-k30Xl4/transcript.jsonl:18) · [Claude check](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-regional-sales-2-9d6sKK/transcript.jsonl:97)\n\n### Re-audit and bias\n\nI replayed both audit versions on all 24 full transcripts. Both matched the saved records exactly. Eight passes stayed clean. Fifteen voids cleared: all 12 Claude runs, Codex visits 1, and Codex sales 2 and 3. The distinction is **execution**. Most flags were regexes, HTML tags or search text. Claude sales 2 and revenue 1 also named genuine `/tmp/axylo-tools` destinations in commands denied before execution. The sixteenth void involved a real lookup: `codex-daily-revenue-3` received “No such file or directory” for both `/AGENTS.md` and `/tmp/AGENTS.md` **during the attempt**. Counting that run was a retrospective eligibility change, recorded in its history. Claude revenue 1 also tried `git log` in its workspace and found no repository. [Denied helper](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-regional-sales-2-9d6sKK/transcript.jsonl:16) · [Missing-file result](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-daily-revenue-3-n1Cfgj/transcript.jsonl:6) · [Original verdict](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-daily-revenue-3-n1Cfgj/run.json:232) · [Git result](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-claude-daily-revenue-1-Rt4jil/transcript.jsonl:7)\n\nAll 24 input and grader hashes matched. Replay restored the saved answers for the 16 voids without another model turn. All 12 Claude charts, its one helper, and the Codex revenue-3 chart readback matched the transcript bytes. The other Codex file-change events record paths rather than file contents, so their provenance rests on the runner’s saved copy. I used the saved Chrome results and reran the read-only summary, whose final line was `rehearsal rule met`. [Restore and hash check](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/reaudit.mjs:30) · [Replay decision](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/reaudit.mjs:69) · [Codex readback](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-daily-revenue-3-n1Cfgj/transcript.jsonl:36)\n\nI found no model-specific cue or scoring preference in the prompts, fixtures or reader grader. The tasks closely follow the getting-started tutorial, the builder was one tested model, and the design reviewer shared its provider. The CLI conditions also differed: the original audit disproportionately voided Claude, the shell permissions differed, and only Codex reported effective `xhigh`. The record supports the release decision without a fresh batch. It cannot establish a model ranking or prove filesystem-enforced docs-only access. [Design review](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/reviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-design.review.md:1) · [Protocol and effort](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/README.md:12) · [Audit limits](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/README.md:26)\n\n### Copy for conformance and stability\n\nOn release candidate `1e93128`, Codex CLI `gpt-6.2-sol` and Claude Code `claude-opus-5-5` each passed three uncoached attempts on each of four fixed line and bar tasks, for 24/24 after transcript re-audit. Each saved answer met the reader checks without error diagnostics on both inputs in Chrome 158 in the Los Angeles and Tokyo time zones. This agent rehearsal provides no estimate of general success and establishes neither human or screen-reader usability nor WCAG conformance.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
