{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '149,188p;215,235p' docs/02-product-spec.md && sed -n '63,90p;130,148p;350,371p' docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md\"","aggregated_output":"### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\n\nLevel decides delivery. **Error**: the chart or a series cannot be drawn; the chart shows its error state. **Warning**: something is not as the author gave it; where that means values were left out or changed (F12), the reader sees the note, and the registry's rows say which codes those are (a warning about an option, a name, a colour or a tick label shows none). **Hint**: advice for the author. Errors and warnings print once per code per page in every build; hints print in development builds only. `strict: true` throws on errors and warnings found while validating a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. A diagnostic that can only arise later (a contrast check after the page's CSS changes, a custom formatter that throws when a table page is built, a label that stops fitting after a resize) is reported when it is found and is never thrown. Every diagnostic names the chart; those about data name the field and the first row indexes in the caller's own data.\n\n| Code | Level | Condition | What is drawn |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| AX100 | Error | Typed columns have different lengths | Error state; the message names the columns and their lengths |\n| AX101 | Error | A named field is not a key of any row | Error state; the message lists the keys that exist and the closest match |\n| AX102 | Warning | A field is absent (`undefined`) in some rows | Gaps |\n| AX103 | Warning | A value is `NaN` or infinite | Gap |\n| AX104 | Hint | Every non-missing value of a value field is a plain decimal string (`'40'`, `'-1.5'`) | Parsed and drawn; the message suggests `parse: 'number'`. An empty string counts as missing |\n| AX105 | Hint | Steps are absent on a regular time, date, wall-clock or number axis | Gaps. When a series would need more absent steps than twice its observations (and more than 1,000), it is treated as irregular instead: no gaps are created and the hint says so |\n| AX106 | Warning | A value field mixes numbers with strings, or holds strings that are not plain decimals (`'1,234'`, `'n/a'`) | The strings are not drawn |\n| AX107 | Warning | A date or time cannot be parsed | That row is not drawn |\n| AX108 | Error | Duplicate x within a series, or duplicate donut labels | That series, or the donut, is not drawn |\n| AX109 | Hint | Numbers on an axis declared `time` look like seconds, or a numeric x looks like epoch milliseconds without `type: 'time'` | As declared |\n| AX110 | Warning | An author domain on bar or area excluded zero | Domain widened to zero |\n| AX111 | Hint | Instants look like calendar dates | As instants, zone shown |\n| AX112 | Hint | Date-time strings have no offset | As written |\n| AX120 | Error | Negative donut value | Error state |\n| AX121 | Warning | More than eight donut slices | Seven largest plus \"Other (n)\" |\n| AX130 | Warning | More than eight series | All series, with dash or marker differences |\n| AX140 | Warning | No `title` and no `labelledBy` | Generated name |\n| AX141 | Warning | Palette below 3:1 on the resolved background | As themed |\n| AX142 | Hint | Plot too small to draw | Summary and table control |\n| AX143 | Hint | Labels hidden after every remedy | Note shown to the reader |\n| AX150 | Warning | Unknown option key | Ignored; the message suggests the closest known key |\n| AX151 | Warning | `append` rows out of order | Accepted through the slower full update |\n| AX131 | Hint | A compact chart has more than one series | Drawn, with dash differences |\n| AX132 | Hint | More bars than pixels along the category axis | Drawn; the message suggests aggregating |\n| AX133 | Hint | A scatter chart has more than 50,000 points | Drawn; the message says drawing may be slow |\n| AX134 | Error | Series × x positions exceeds 20,000,000 (long rows with many series and little shared x) | Error state; the message gives both counts and suggests fewer series or a scatter chart |\n| AX152 | Warning | `timeZone` is not a zone the browser knows | The viewer's zone is used |\n| AX160 | Warning | A custom formatter threw | The default format is used for that value. A value formatter that throws changes how a value reads, so the reader's note (F12) is shown; a tick formatter that throws only changes an axis label, which is not a value left out or changed, so it is reported without a note (and so the note can never change which ticks fit) |\n| AX199 | Error | An error was thrown while Axylo was building the chart: by Axylo itself, or by code the author supplied that Axylo was running (a row getter, a callback; a formatter that throws is AX160, and the default format is used) | Error state with a generic message; the error is passed to `onDiagnostic` and printed |\n\nExpected results for the Phase 1 fixtures: the combined `dirty` fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`) yields AX102, AX103 and AX106 once each, draws Mon–Tue as a segment and Sun as a point, and shows the F12 note. `dirty-string` (one `'40'` among numbers) yields AX106. A column made only of numeric strings yields AX104 and is drawn in full. `dirty-null` yields nothing.\n\nThe split between AX104 and AX106 answers a reviewer's objection that common back ends return counts as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery): a whole column of plain decimal strings is unambiguous and is parsed; a stray string among numbers is a bug and is not.\n\n### 3.8 What the evidence will and will not show\n\nAutomated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast, text. They do not establish that a screen-reader user finds a chart understandable. 1.0 has no evidence for that: no screen reader was run against it, not even by the developer (the owner's decision, 2026-11-18; the rehearsal on five reader and browser pairs that earlier drafts of this section planned was not done). Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, so no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made, and the conformance table says both things in those words. Reviewers proposed participant testing as a release gate; it is recorded here as a known limit instead.\n\n## 4. Acceptance criteria: the developer's experience\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| DX1 | The first chart takes ten lines or fewer, including import, by npm and by script tag. No required option besides data and fields. | Two CI tests that render the documented snippet from the packed distribution | D1 |\n| DX2 | One logical table, three ways to pass it: long rows, wide rows, typed columns. The three forms of each fixture produce the same normalized data and the same diagnostics, with row indexes in the caller's own form. | Equivalence tests | D2 |\n| DX3 | With typed rows, a misspelled field, a text field on a value axis, wide `y` combined with `series`, and an option that does not apply to the chart type all fail to compile. At run time, for untyped rows, the same mistakes raise AX101, AX106 and AX150. | [docs/api/examples.ts](api/examples.ts) compiles and each marked mistake fails (already checked with TypeScript 7.0.2); runtime tests per code | D5 |\n| DX4 | Diagnostics follow the registry in section 3.5: stable code, chart name, field and rows where they apply, the fix, and a documentation page per code. | Message snapshots; link checker | R5, D5 |\n| DX5 | Three methods change a chart: `update` changes options, `append` adds rows, `zoom` sets the view. They apply in call order. Validation and diagnostics are synchronous; drawing is deferred to the next frame; marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer always switch to a new state together. `destroy()` releases everything and cancels pending work. No `resize()` exists. | Sequence tests: replace then append then zoom in one turn; a rejected `strict` update leaves the last valid chart; destroy before the frame; 1,000 create–destroy cycles show no heap growth trend (P7) | D3, R16 |\n| DX6 | Colors, font and spacing are CSS custom properties with documented names; the chart inherits `font-family` and `color`. `colors` pins a color to a series name so the same series has the same color on every chart. There is no theme object and no positional palette option. | Theming tests, including a 24-tile page where series subsets differ | R10 |\n| DX7 | Zero runtime dependencies (reconsidered only with a measured comparison). Size targets for 1.0, set by the owner on 2026-11-18 after measurement: 116 KiB gzipped for everything and 98 KiB for a page that uses only `line`, CSS included. (History: the first targets, 40 and 25 KiB, were written before anything was built; 96 and 80 were set the same day from a first analysis, `reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/size.analysis.md`; the correctness passes that followed added about 11 KiB, and a second measured analysis, `reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/size2.analysis.md`, showed 96 and 80 can only be met by making streaming an opt-in import, a public API change the owner left for after 1.0. That change, worth about 15 KiB, is the first size item after 1.0.) | size-limit in CI on the packed distribution | D4 |\n| DX8 | Importing in Node without a DOM does not throw, and `validate()` runs there. Creating, destroying and creating again on the same element works and leaks nothing (the sequence a framework's strict or development mode produces). Works under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline` or `unsafe-eval`, served with real headers. Labels and formatter output are inserted as text, never as HTML. A target that matches no element throws an error that names the selector. | CI tests | D1 |\n| DX9 | Semantic versioning. A deprecated API keeps working for at least one minor release and six months. At most one major version a year. The previous release's documented examples still compile and run against each release candidate. | Release policy; API report diff; upgrade test | D6 |\n| DX10 | Every runnable example in the documentation is compiled and rendered in CI from the packed distribution. | Docs build | D1 |\n| DX11 | MIT license for everything, including zoom and accessibility. Third-party notices are verified at release. | Repository; release check | D7 |\n| DX12 | A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence. | Scripted rehearsal at each release | D1, 1.2 |\n\n### 1.3 Fields\n\nA field is a key of the row, or an object when it needs more. The key is checked against the row type by role: a value axis accepts number fields; text fields are accepted there only with `parse: 'number'`.\n\n```ts\nline(el, {\n  data: samples,\n  x: { field: 'ts', type: 'time', timeZone: 'UTC' },\n  y: { field: 'sessions', label: 'Sessions', unit: 'sessions', tickFormat: { notation: 'compact' } },\n});\n\n// counts that a database driver returns as strings\nbar(el, { data: pgRows, x: 'day', y: { field: 'count', parse: 'number', missing: 'zero' } });\n```\n\n| Option | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `field` | Key of the row. |\n| `label`, `unit` | Axis title; name and unit in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n| `type` (x) | `'date'`, `'time'`, `'number'`, `'category'`. Inferred when omitted, except that a number is never inferred to be an instant (F4). |\n| `timeZone` (x) | For instants: `'local'` (default), `'UTC'`, or an IANA zone. |\n| `format`, `tickFormat` | Readouts and ticks are formatted separately (F6). |\n| `scale` (value) | `'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1). Declared, never inferred (F7). |\n| `missing` (value) | `'gap'` (default) or `'zero'` (F2). |\n| `parse` (value) | `'number'`, for a text field holding numbers (AX104, AX106). |\n| `domain` | Author bounds. Widened to zero on bar and area (F1). |\n\n### 1.4 Common options\n### 1.6 Diagnostics\n\nThe registry, with each code's level and outcome, is in the spec (section 3.5). Example for the Phase 1 combined dirty fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`):\n\n```\n[axylo AX103] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" has 1 value that is not a finite number (NaN at row 3). Shown as a gap.\n  Check the calculation that produced it, or send null for \"no data\".\n[axylo AX106] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" mixes numbers with 1 string (\"40\" at row 4). The string is not plotted.\n  Convert it to a number where the data is produced.\n[axylo AX102] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" is absent in 1 row (row 5). Shown as a gap.\n  Send null if the value is known to be missing.\n```\n\nThe reader of that chart sees \"Values not shown: 3. See table.\" (F12).\n\n`validate(type, options)` runs the same normalization without a DOM and returns the diagnostics, so a server, a test or a coding agent can check a chart before rendering it.\n\nDevelopment and production flavours are selected through package `exports` conditions. The script-tag distribution ships `axylo.js` (development) and `axylo.min.js` (production). Errors and warnings print once per code per page in both; hints only in development.\n\n### M7. Distribution and documentation (ceiling: 8 days)\n\n- Script-tag builds; documentation site; migration notes from Chart.js and Recharts. (No framework adapter in 1.0.)\n\nOutcomes:\n- The packed tarball installed into two clean fixtures (npm with a bundler, script tag): the first chart works in ten lines or fewer in each (DX1).\n- Node import and `validate()` without a DOM; strict CSP with real headers (DX8).\n- Size measured on the packed distribution for line-only, full and script-tag cases, CSS included (DX7).\n- Every runnable docs example compiled and rendered from the packed distribution (DX10).\n- The agent rehearsal (DX12), recorded as rehearsal.\n\n### M8. Release (ceiling: 10 days)\n\n- Beta under a `next` tag; issues from real use fixed; API frozen through the API report.\n- The Phase 1 comparison regenerated with Axylo beside the ten libraries, with the same harness and the corrections from the review, and published with its limits.\n- Real Safari on macOS and iOS checked by hand.\n- Independent review of the acceptance evidence before 1.0 is tagged, by a reviewer who did not write the code under review.\n\nOutcomes: every acceptance-matrix row is pass or not applicable with a reason; changelog, upgrade policy and third-party notices published.\n\nPublishing to npm, tagging and pushing are separate, explicit decisions by the owner at this point; the plan does not assume them.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat rehearsal/test/fixtures.test.mjs rehearsal/fixtures/daily-revenue* && cat rehearsal/README.md && rg -n 'alternate|expected\\\\.json' rehearsal -g '*.mjs' -g '\"'!**/records/**'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat rehearsal/test/fixtures.test.mjs rehearsal/fixtures/daily-revenue* && cat rehearsal/README.md && rg -n 'alternate|expected\\\\.json' rehearsal -g '*.mjs' -g '\"'!**/records/**'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"import { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';\nimport fc from 'fast-check';\nimport { readJson, taskFiles } from '../files.mjs';\nimport { calendarDate, compareHeaders, compareRows } from '../table.mjs';\n\nconst specs = [\n  ['daily-visits', [2026, 3, 1, 30], ['visits'], [6, 12, 26]],\n  ['signups', [2026, 4, 6, 7], ['signpus'], []],\n  ['daily-revenue', [2026, 5, 1, 8], ['revenue'], [4]],\n  ['regional-sales', null, ['north', 'central', 'west'], []],\n];\n\nfunction quarterOrdinal(label) {\n  const [, year, quarter] = /^(\\d{4}) Q([1-4])$/.exec(label);\n  return Number(year) * 4 + Number(quarter);\n}\n\ndescribe('fixture expectations', () => {\n  it.each(specs)('%s follows the calendar or quarters and preserves the source values in both inputs', async (task, period, keys, missing) => {\n    const files = taskFiles(task);\n    const [primary, expected, alternate, alternateExpected] = await Promise.all(\n      [files.fixture, files.expected, files.alternate, files.alternateExpected].map(readJson));\n    expect(alternateExpected.series).toEqual(expected.series);\n    expect(alternateExpected.rows.map(row => row[0])).toEqual(expected.rows.map(row => row[0]));\n    for (const [i, [day, ...values]] of expected.rows.entries()) {\n      expect(alternateExpected.rows[i][0]).toBe(day);\n      for (const [j, value] of values.entries()) {\n        if (value !== null) expect(alternateExpected.rows[i][j + 1]).not.toBe(value);\n      }\n    }\n    for (const [data, answer] of [[primary, expected], [alternate, alternateExpected]]) {\n      if (!period) {\n        expect(data.every(row => keys.every(key => typeof row[key] === 'string'))).toBe(true);\n        const quarters = data.toSorted((a, b) => quarterOrdinal(a.quarter) - quarterOrdinal(b.quarter));\n        expect(quarters.map(row => row.quarter)).not.toEqual(data.map(row => row.quarter));\n        expect(answer.rows).toEqual(quarters.map(row => [row.quarter, ...keys.map(key => Number(row[key]))]));\n        continue;\n      }\n\n      const [year, month, start, count] = period;\n      const days = Array.from({ length: count }, (_, index) =>\n        new Date(Date.UTC(year, month - 1, start + index)).toISOString().split('T')[0]);\n      const observations = new Map(data.map(row => [row.day.split('T')[0], row]));\n      expect(observations.size).toBe(data.length);\n      expect(data.length).toBe(days.length - missing.length);\n      expect(days.filter(day => !observations.has(day)).map(day => Number(day.slice(-2)))).toEqual(missing);\n      const omitted = data.flatMap(row => keys.filter(key => !Object.hasOwn(row, key)));\n      expect(omitted).toHaveLength(task === 'signups' && data === alternate ? 1 : 0);\n      expect(answer.valuesNotShown ?? 0).toBe(omitted.length);\n      expect(answer.rows).toEqual(days.map(day => {\n        const row = observations.get(day);\n        return [day, ...keys.map(key => row && Object.hasOwn(row, key) ? Number(row[key]) : null)];\n      }));\n      if (missing.length) {\n        expect(data.map(row => row.day)).not.toEqual(data.map(row => row.day).sort());\n        expect(data.every(row => row.day.endsWith('T00:00:00Z'))).toBe(true);\n      }\n    }\n  });\n\n  it('accepts formatted numbers and omitted gaps, and rejects a zero substituted for any gap', () => {\n    fc.assert(fc.property(fc.array(fc.integer({ min: -1000000, max: 1000000 }), { minLength: 1, maxLength: 12 }), values => {\n      const expected = {\n        x: 'category', series: [{ label: 'Sales' }],\n        rows: [['Missing', null], ...values.map((value, i) => [String(i), value])],\n      };\n      const format = new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US');\n      const displayed = values.map((value, i) => [String(i), format.format(value)]);\n      expect(compareRows(expected, displayed).pass).toBe(true);\n      expect(compareRows(expected, [['Missing', 'No data'], ...displayed]).pass).toBe(true);\n      expect(compareRows(expected, [['Missing', '0'], ...displayed]).pass).toBe(false);\n    }), { numRuns: 1000 });\n  });\n\n  it.each(['Apr 6, 2026', 'April 6 2026', '06 April 2026', '6 Apr. 2026', '2026-04-06', '4/6/2026',\n    'Monday, April 6, 2026'])('reads %s as the same date', text => {\n    expect(calendarDate(text)).toBe('2026-04-06');\n  });\n\n  it.each(['Apr 6, 2026, 9:00 AM GMT+9', '2026-04-06T00:00:00Z', '31 April 2026'])('rejects %s as a calendar date', text => {\n    expect(calendarDate(text)).toBeNull();\n  });\n\n  it('compares every label and unit and accepts reordered series with their values', () => {\n    const expected = {\n      x: 'date', series: [{ label: 'North', unit: 'USD' }, { label: 'West' }],\n      rows: [['2026-04-06', 1250.5, 12]],\n    };\n    expect(compareHeaders(expected, ['Day', 'west', 'North (USD)'])).toMatchObject({ pass: true, order: [2, 1] });\n    expect(compareHeaders(expected, ['Day', 'West', 'North (kg)']).pass).toBe(false);\n    expect(compareHeaders(expected, ['Day', 'West', 'North']).pass).toBe(false);\n    expect(compareHeaders(expected, ['Day', 'West (kg)', 'North (USD)']).pass).toBe(false);\n    expect(compareRows(expected, [['06 April 2026', '12', '1,250.50 USD']], [2, 1]).pass).toBe(true);\n    expect(compareRows(expected, [['06 April 2026', '12', '1,250.50']], [2, 1]).pass).toBe(false);\n  });\n});\n{\n  \"title\": \"Daily store revenue\",\n  \"type\": \"line\",\n  \"x\": \"date\",\n  \"series\": [\n    {\n      \"label\": \"Revenue\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rows\": [\n    [\"2026-05-01\", 1425.25],\n    [\"2026-05-02\", 1082.5],\n    [\"2026-05-03\", 1047.75],\n    [\"2026-05-04\", null],\n    [\"2026-05-05\", 1837.5],\n    [\"2026-05-06\", 1649.75],\n    [\"2026-05-07\", 1728.5],\n    [\"2026-05-08\", 1941.25]\n  ]\n}\n[\n  {\"day\": \"2026-05-07T00:00:00Z\", \"revenue\": \"1728.5\"},\n  {\"day\": \"2026-05-03T00:00:00Z\", \"revenue\": \"1047.75\"},\n  {\"day\": \"2026-05-05T00:00:00Z\", \"revenue\": 1837.5},\n  {\"day\": \"2026-05-01T00:00:00Z\", \"revenue\": \"1425.25\"},\n  {\"day\": \"2026-05-08T00:00:00Z\", \"revenue\": 1941.25},\n  {\"day\": \"2026-05-02T00:00:00Z\", \"revenue\": \"1082.5\"},\n  {\"day\": \"2026-05-06T00:00:00Z\", \"revenue\": \"1649.75\"}\n]\n{\n  \"title\": \"Daily store revenue\",\n  \"type\": \"line\",\n  \"x\": \"date\",\n  \"series\": [\n    {\n      \"label\": \"Revenue\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rows\": [\n    [\"2026-05-01\", 1302.25],\n    [\"2026-05-02\", 984.5],\n    [\"2026-05-03\", 899.75],\n    [\"2026-05-04\", null],\n    [\"2026-05-05\", 1576.5],\n    [\"2026-05-06\", 1438.75],\n    [\"2026-05-07\", 1693.5],\n    [\"2026-05-08\", 1705.25]\n  ]\n}\n[\n  { \"day\": \"2026-05-06T00:00:00Z\", \"revenue\": \"1438.75\" },\n  { \"day\": \"2026-05-02T00:00:00Z\", \"revenue\": \"984.5\" },\n  { \"day\": \"2026-05-08T00:00:00Z\", \"revenue\": 1705.25 },\n  { \"day\": \"2026-05-01T00:00:00Z\", \"revenue\": \"1302.25\" },\n  { \"day\": \"2026-05-05T00:00:00Z\", \"revenue\": 1576.5 },\n  { \"day\": \"2026-05-03T00:00:00Z\", \"revenue\": \"899.75\" },\n  { \"day\": \"2026-05-07T00:00:00Z\", \"revenue\": \"1693.5\" }\n]\n# DX12 agent rehearsal\n\nUse one records directory per release rehearsal. Build the published docs and pack the release candidate before running. The harness reads `apps/docs/dist` and `tests/dist/.packed/axylo-0.0.0.tgz`. Installations of Codex CLI, Claude Code, system Chrome, and the repository development dependencies are required. Codex uses the file login at `~/.codex/auth.json`. Claude uses `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` when nonempty and otherwise uses the machine's OAuth login at `~/.claude/.credentials.json`.\n\n```sh\nnode rehearsal/run.mjs --attempts 3 --parallel 2 --records /tmp/axylo-release-rehearsal\nnode rehearsal/summary.mjs --records /tmp/axylo-release-rehearsal\n```\n\n## Release protocol\n\n- Use the fixed prompts for `daily-visits`, `regional-sales`, `signups`, and `daily-revenue`. Run three fresh, uncoached attempts per task with each of Codex CLI `gpt-6.2-sol` and Claude Code `claude-opus-5-5`. Both request `xhigh` effort and have 20 minutes per attempt. Codex reports the observed model and effort through its rollout. Claude reports the observed model in assistant messages. The invocation records the requested `--effort` and the stream leaves effective effort unknown. An observed Claude version suffix is retained and must agree across counted runs for that agent.\n- Give each agent the same prompt bytes, published docs, installed package and primary data. Allow reading inputs, writing the answer and running documented Node self-checks within the time limit. Grade after the CLI exits. Keep grading data, expected results and grader output out of the staged inputs and give the agent no feedback or follow-up.\n- An invocation fills open slots up to `--attempts` per selected cell. The default is three. `--parallel` caps concurrent attempts and defaults to one. Omit `--agent` and `--task` to run all eight cells. Use one runner invocation at a time for a records directory. `--workdir` selects the scratch parent outside the repository and defaults to the system temporary directory. Records default to `rehearsal/records/`.\n- Keep passes, failures and timeouts as counted outcomes. Only voids and infrastructure failures leave slots open. A CLI authentication or provider failure or an exit before a model turn is an infrastructure failure. The runner records the reason and uses saved transcripts to identify older infrastructure failures. Rerun the same batch to fill open slots after inspecting the saved reason. The new record links to the record it replaces. A harness timeout still counts when truncation leaves the audit unverifiable and the transcript has no recorded path violation or evidence of a CLI infrastructure failure.\n- The owner decided on 2026-11-19 that the 1.0 rehearsal rule requires at least two passes in three counted slots in each of the eight agent × task cells. The summary shows the counts and rule status for each cell, the batch totals, and the recorded failures. It also checks that counted records use matching docs, package, task inputs, grader, tool versions and observed model IDs. Keep the release candidate and CLI installations fixed while collecting a batch. An empty summary displays zero counts for all cells.\n- Report this as “agent rehearsal: these prompts, these models, Chrome; not user evidence”. With three independent runs per cell, the two-sided exact 95% binomial interval is 9.43% to 99.16% for two successes and 29.24% to 100% for three. The counts apply to these line and bar tasks in Chrome and do not establish general success rates or human usability.\n\n## Isolation and audit\n\nEach attempt gets a newly created directory outside the repository with a fresh home, temporary storage and workspace. The workspace contains copies of the published docs, the unpacked package, primary data and prompt, and a writable location for `chart.js` and its helpers. The docs retain published examples and exclude internal source snapshots. The runner applies `chmod -R a-w` to `docs/` and `node_modules/` and removes write permission from the fixture, prompt and package manifest. These file modes protect against accidental writes. A sufficiently privileged or same-owner subprocess can still change modes or replace inputs. The runner verifies staged input hashes before grading, and the browser grader supplies canonical data from the harness.\n\n- **Codex CLI 0.170.0.** The runner assigns a fresh `CODEX_HOME` and symlinks only `auth.json` from the real login. It uses `--ignore-user-config` and `--ignore-rules`, disables apps, plugins, memory and hooks, skips host skill discovery, and disables web search. It uses `-s workspace-write`, `-a never`, and `sandbox_workspace_write.network_access=false`. The CLI help describes `--sandbox` as selecting the sandbox for “model-generated shell commands” and says `--ignore-user-config` still uses `CODEX_HOME` for auth. Workspace-write applies write and network restrictions to agent commands. It allows host reads, and Codex's shell can run more than Node. The prompt and audit constrain use of that wider surface.\n- **Claude Code 2.1.327.** Each attempt has a fresh `HOME` and `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`. With an API key, the runner passes it through and uses `--bare`, which skips OAuth, auto-memory and `CLAUDE.md` discovery. For OAuth the only initial config entry is a `.credentials.json` symlink into the real home. The runner creates and removes the symlink without opening the credential file. The fresh config and staged workspace contain no local Claude customizations or saved memory. Without `--bare`, `--safe-mode` disables `CLAUDE.md` discovery, skills, installed plugins, hooks and other customizations, and `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AUTO_MEMORY=1` turns off auto-memory. Built-in plugins, admin-managed settings and managed plugin hooks can still apply.\n- Both Claude modes keep `--restricted`, which ignores user, project and local settings and confines file tools to working directories. `--strict-mcp-config` limits MCP to the supplied empty configuration, `--disable-slash-commands` disables skills and commands, and `--no-session-persistence` turns off session saves in print mode. The built-in allowlist exposes file tools and Bash, with `--allowedTools` limiting Bash permission to `node …` and `dontAsk` denying other calls. `--permission-prompts none` denies anything that would prompt. Web tools and Chrome are absent. A Node subprocess can have host filesystem and network access through the operating system.\n- Both CLIs need their provider transport. These CLI controls give fresh session state and restrict configured tools. They do not provide a filesystem namespace or a transport-only network policy for Claude's Node subprocess. For strictly enforced docs-only reads and network isolation, use external isolation that enforces those boundaries for both CLIs. Keep host managed policies fixed and record them with the rehearsal if they apply.\n\nThe audit requires a parseable transcript with the CLI's start and terminal events. Empty, malformed, or truncated transcripts are **unverifiable**. The audit takes paths from file-tool inputs and literal shell file operands, including redirects and paths relative to an explicit working directory or a `cd` command. It skips command bodies blocked by the CLI before execution, file contents, inline programs, search patterns, tool output and assistant prose. The audit can resolve symlinks when it has filesystem evidence. “No findings” describes the transcript only. A subprocess's actual reads, dynamically constructed paths and fetches from Node code are outside the transcript's coverage.\n\nThe attempt's workspace, fresh home and config, and temporary storage are all inside its run directory. Recorded accesses to the repository, the real home, another attempt or the linked login void an attempt, even for missing files and after a timeout. System tools are allowed. Other outside paths are recorded under “looked outside the workspace,” with each path's existence. Only existing paths in that group void the attempt. Excluded tools also make a completed audit ineligible for grading.\n\nEach record stores `run.json` with the CLI version, arguments, requested and observed model metadata, elapsed time and status, and SHA-256 hashes of both inputs, their expected results, the prompt, copied docs, packed library and grader. Claude records include `claudeMode` as `bare` or `fresh-config`. The record also stores the transcript, stderr, answer files if present, and the grade and Chrome version if the answer reached Chrome. Audit version 2 records the original workspace and the filesystem observations used to resolve paths. The disposable workspace and home, including the credential symlink, are removed after saving the record. An interrupted batch may leave an incomplete record or scratch directory.\n\n## Reader grading and verification\n\nThe grader uses clean headless Chrome contexts in Los Angeles and Tokyo with English (US) as the page locale. Each answer must handle the original and changed-value input. The alternate signups input has an absent value field that should produce an omission note. Dates are graded as calendar days, including unambiguous month-first and day-first forms. Series labels must match the requested fields. The fixtures declare no measurement unit or currency. The grader opens the standard table control, checks caption, semantic headers and rows, reads the plotted x order and SVG stroke breaks, samples the first calendar date through the plot control, and checks title clipping and omission counts in both the visible note and the accessibility tree.\n\nRe-auditing is allowed because the audit applies deterministic rules to the saved transcript and its path context. Version 1 records lack saved filesystem observations, so replay checks outside file existence on the host and can only resolve symlinks still present there. Run the command on the original host with the release candidate's built docs and packed library available. Use one writer at a time for the records directory.\n\n```sh\nnode rehearsal/reaudit.mjs --records rehearsal/records/release-1e93128\nnode rehearsal/summary.mjs --records rehearsal/records/release-1e93128\n```\n\nThe command re-audits every record and uses the runner's grader for voids eligible for grading. It prepares a fresh workspace, verifies the input and grader hashes in `run.json`, then restores and grades the saved answer. A hash mismatch or a browser infrastructure error leaves the previous verdict in place and causes exit 2. The transcript and answer stay intact. Every updated record keeps the previous verdict, audit version and date in `run.json` under `history`. A re-audited record entering a counted slot keeps its original attempt number. The summary uses the latest verdicts and reports how many records were re-audited.\n\nFor a single prepared workspace or the test suite, use the following commands.\n\n```sh\nnode rehearsal/grade.mjs --task daily-visits --workdir /path/to/prepared/workspace\nnpx --no-install vitest run --root rehearsal --exclude '**/*.browser.test.mjs'\nnpx --no-install vitest run --root rehearsal\n```\n\nThe first Vitest command runs fixture, comparator, audit and local-runner tests without Chrome. The second also runs the browser references and negative controls. The runner exits 0 when all newly scheduled attempts pass or there are no open slots, 1 for any new failed, timed-out or void attempt, and 2 for setup or infrastructure errors. Use the summary to evaluate the whole release rehearsal.\nrehearsal/protocol.mjs:55:  const { alternate, expected, alternateExpected } = taskFiles(task);\nrehearsal/protocol.mjs:58:    alternate: fileHash(alternate),\nrehearsal/protocol.mjs:60:    alternateExpected: fileHash(alternateExpected),\nrehearsal/files.mjs:20:    expected: join(rehearsal, 'fixtures', task + '.expected.json'),\nrehearsal/files.mjs:21:    alternate: join(rehearsal, 'fixtures', task + '.alternate.json'),\nrehearsal/files.mjs:22:    alternateExpected: join(rehearsal, 'fixtures', task + '.alternate.expected.json'),\nrehearsal/grade.mjs:220:    ['alternate', taskPaths.alternate, taskPaths.alternateExpected],\nrehearsal/test/grade.browser.test.mjs:86:    ['primary', LA], ['primary', Tokyo], ['alternate', LA], ['alternate', Tokyo],\nrehearsal/test/grade.browser.test.mjs:110:    ['primary', LA], ['primary', Tokyo], ['alternate', LA], ['alternate', Tokyo],\nrehearsal/test/grade.browser.test.mjs:169:  expect(result.timezones.filter(zone => zone.input === 'alternate').every(zone => !criterion(zone, 'table rows').pass)).toBe(true);\nrehearsal/test/runner.test.mjs:397